Subject: [PATCH 3/7] decoder: clamp map allocation against forged map length

Decoder.DecodeMap and Decoder.DecodeUntypedMap called

    make(map[K]V, n)

with n taken directly from the wire-format map16 / map32 length. A
hostile map32 header (for example, 0xdf 0xff 0xff 0xff 0xff) caused
the runtime to allocate a multi-gigabyte hashtable up front before
the underlying short input failed, which is a reliable single-packet
OOM.

This patch clamps the initial sizing hint to maxMapSize unless the
caller has explicitly opted out via Decoder.DisableAllocLimit(true).
The map continues to grow naturally as real entries arrive.

Backwards compatible: same exported API, same wire format, same
semantics for well-formed input.

Apply from the package root:

    patch -p1 < 0003-decoder-clamp-map-allocation.patch

---
 decode_map.go | 14 ++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

--- a/decode_map.go
+++ b/decode_map.go
@@ -157,7 +157,14 @@
 		return nil, nil
 	}
 
-	m := make(map[string]interface{}, n)
+	// Clamp the initial map sizing hint so a forged map32 length cannot
+	// trigger a multi-gigabyte hashtable allocation up front. Real
+	// entries still grow the map as input arrives.
+	hint := n
+	if d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag == 0 && hint > maxMapSize {
+		hint = maxMapSize
+	}
+	m := make(map[string]interface{}, hint)
 
 	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
 		mk, err := d.DecodeString()
@@ -184,7 +191,13 @@
 		return nil, nil
 	}
 
-	m := make(map[interface{}]interface{}, n)
+	// See DecodeMap(): clamp the initial sizing hint to defeat a forged
+	// map32 length.
+	hint := n
+	if d.flags&disableAllocLimitFlag == 0 && hint > maxMapSize {
+		hint = maxMapSize
+	}
+	m := make(map[interface{}]interface{}, hint)
 
 	for i := 0; i < n; i++ {
 		mk, err := d.decodeInterfaceCond()
